course: dictionary
lesson: 1814
title: Clump: a group, a lump, or a heavy step
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «A clump of daisies» is a small group of flowers growing close together. «A clump of mud» is a lump of mud stuck together. Same word, different meaning.
explain: Look at the words around clump. If they are about plants or trees, clump means a group growing close together. If they are about mud, hair or milk, clump means a lump stuck together. The clue words do the work for you.
explain: «He clumped up the stairs in his boots» shows that clump can also be a verb. It means to walk with slow, heavy, noisy steps. Check it by asking: is someone walking, and does it sound heavy and noisy? If yes, clump means heavy steps.
fact: A clump is a small group of plants or trees growing close together.
fact: A clump can also be a lump or mass of something stuck together, like «a clump of hair» or «clumps of mud».
fact: To clump together means to stick together in lumps or gather in a group.
fact: To clump means to walk with slow, heavy, noisy steps, as in «clumping footsteps».
fact: In cooking, flour can clump if you don't stir it, and milk proteins clump into lumps when acid is added.
exercise: «She saw a clump of daisies by the gate.» What does clump mean here? | *A small group of plants growing close together | A shiny coin | A heavy, noisy step | A sharp, hard stone
exercise: «Acid makes milk proteins clump into lumps.» What does clump mean here? | *Stick together in lumps | Walk slowly across the kitchen | Fly up into the air | Turn bright red
exercise: «He clumped up the stairs in his boots.» How did he move? | Backwards, one step at a time | *Slowly and heavily, with noisy steps | Quietly on tiptoe | Fast and light, like a cat
test: «There was mud in clumps on his boots.» Which meaning fits clumps here? | Tiny drops of rain | *Lumps of mud stuck together | A sharp smell | A group of trees
test: Jayden says clump only ever means a group of plants. What is the answer? | Yes, clump means a tree trunk | Yes, clump only ever means plants | *No, clump can also mean a lump of something or a heavy walk | No, clump means to run very fast
exam: Which sentence uses clump as a verb, meaning to stick together? | «A clump of grass grew by the wall.» | «A clump of hair fell out.» | *«Flour clumps if you do not stir it.» | «The clump of frogspawn wobbled in the pond.»
